================================================================================
EXCEPTIONS AND THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS
================================================================================

1. QRISK3-2017 — art_cds/calculators/qrisk3_raw.py

   This file is a translation of the QRISK3-2017 C source, copyright 2017
   ClinRisk Ltd., and is licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License,
   version 3 or later — NOT under the MIT licence above. See
   https://www.gnu.org/licenses/lgpl-3.0.html and https://qrisk.org.

   ClinRisk's additional terms require that the disclaimer reproduced in that
   file accompanies any risk score the code generates. The application displays
   it alongside every QRISK3 result. ClinRisk stress that it is the end user's
   responsibility to check that this implementation produces the same results as
   the original code at https://qrisk.org; it is validated against the 97
   reference scores in tests/data/qrisk3_reference.json.

2. Summaries of Product Characteristics (SmPCs)

   SmPC text is the copyright of the respective marketing authorisation holders
   and is published on the electronic medicines compendium (emc,
   https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc). No SmPC text is distributed with this
   repository: `python -m tools.fetch_smpc` downloads it into
   art_cds/data/smpc/, which is excluded from version control. Review emc's
   terms before redistributing any copy you create.

3. BHIVA guidelines

   Guideline text is the copyright of the British HIV Association
   (https://bhiva.org). No guideline text is distributed with this repository:
   `python -m tools.fetch_guidelines` downloads it into
   art_cds/data/guidelines/, which is excluded from version control. It is used
   only to verify that statements in the knowledge base match their source.
   Short quotations appear in the source code as verification patterns and as
   citations.

4. Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database (HIVdb)

   The optional resistance-scoring integration calls the public HIVdb web
   service at https://hivdb.stanford.edu. It is off by default and is subject to
   Stanford's own terms of use.

Nothing in this licence grants permission to use the clinical content for the
treatment of patients: see docs/clinical-safety/README.md for the intended use
and regulatory position.
